 # Batch Chorus and Tattler interim messages outside Pi steering queue
 
-Problem: inbound Tattler/Chorus messages received while an agent is busy currently risk partial context under Pi's one-at-a-time steering mode, and queued custom messages can be dropped if abort clears Pi's steer/followUp queues before they emit message_start/message_end.
-
-Design direction: make Tattler, not Chorus, own a pending inbox for busy-time inbound messages. Real inbound payloads stay out of Pi's clearable queues while busy. A shared drainPendingInboxToBatch helper builds one canonical custom-message batch.
-
-Drain callers:
-- before_agent_start drains for normal user prompts, including user abort + resume.
-- the cooperative receive flow drains directly into a batch custom message and triggers that batch turn after agent_end/idle.
-
-Acceptance criteria:
-- Busy-time inbound messages are stored/deduped outside Pi steer/followUp queues.
-- Normal next prompts drain pending messages via before_agent_start.
-- Synthetic receive turns use the same batch helper and do not depend on a fake user prompt solely to reach before_agent_start.
-- User abort + next prompt recovers queued messages even if the model never called tattler_receive.
-- One-at-a-time queue mode no longer serializes room bursts into partial context.
-- Pending messages are cleared only after successful injection/persistence.
-- Tests cover duplicate delivery, abort recovery, and batch rendering.
-
+Updated again after source review: before_agent_start only runs through normal prompt/sendUserMessage paths, not pi.sendMessage(customMessage,{triggerTurn:true}). Therefore before_agent_start is a user-resume drain path, not universal. The universal invariant should be shared drain helper/schema. User prompt path: before_agent_start calls drainPendingInboxToBatch() and returns a canonical persistent custom batch message. Synthetic receive path: tattler_receive terminates; agent_end defers until idle; then calls the same drain helper and starts a custom-message turn via pi.sendMessage(batchCustomMessage,{triggerTurn:true}). Do not use sendUserMessage for synthetic receive because it renders as Doug/user and has misleading semantics. Both paths produce the same batch content/details/batchId and persist as custom_message; only entrypoint/order differs.
